page ref_container {
  title = "Lab file: container and its children"
  chapter_header "Lab file: container and its children" {
    kicker       = "Reference · reference"
    reading_time = "10 min read"
    updated      = "2026-10-30"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<DOC
    This chapter is the field-by-field reference for the `container {}`
    block and the children only a container carries: `env`, `volume`, `port`
    and `healthcheck`. A container also takes `login {}`, `nic {}`,
    `provision {}` and `playbook {}`, which mean the same thing as on a VM and
    are documented in [vm and its children](ref_vm). It may carry the `@dev`
    decorator described there too. What a container is, and how it runs as a
    micro-VM, is in [Lab containers](guide_containers).
  DOC

  h2 "container {}"
  p <<DOC
    An OCI container run inside a micro-VM. It attaches to segments exactly
    like a VM, registers in DNS as `<name>.<lab>.<suffix>`, and joins the
    same `depends_on` start order and `depends_on_provision` provisioning
    order as a VM. A container with no NICs is air-gapped but
    still reachable with `exec` and `cp` over the agent channel.
  DOC
  code wcl { source = <<'SRC'
container "<name>" {
  image      = "nginx:1.27"
  mode       = :workload
  entrypoint = ["/bin/sh", "-c"]
  command    = ["…"]
  workdir    = "/app"
  user       = "1000:1000"
  profile    = "container"
  cpus       = 1
  memory     = 256MiB
  depends_on = ["db"]
  depends_on_provision = ["db"]
  clock      = "host"
  nic         { … }
  env         { … }
  volume      { … }
  port        { … }
  healthcheck { … }
  login "…"   { … }
  provision "…" { … }
  playbook "…"  { … }
}
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`name`" | "utf8 (label)" | "required" | "Container name, a DNS label, unique per lab. VMs and containers share one namespace; the inline block label." |
      | "`image`" | "utf8" | "required" | "OCI image reference, for example `nginx:1.27` or `ghcr.io/owner/app@sha256:…`." |
      | "`mode`" | "symbol" | "`:workload`" | "`:workload` starts the OCI process; `:idle` keeps the micro-VM available for exec without running it." |
      | "`entrypoint`" | "list<utf8>" | "image default" | "Override the image entrypoint, in exec form." |
      | "`command`" | "list<utf8>" | "image default" | "Override the image cmd, in exec form." |
      | "`workdir`" | "utf8" | "image default" | "Working directory inside the container." |
      | "`user`" | "utf8" | "image default" | "User to run as: `uid[:gid]` or a name from the image." |
      | "`profile`" | "utf8" | "none" | "Guest profile supplying micro-VM hardware defaults, for example `container`." |
      | "`cpus`" | "i64" | "from profile" | "vCPU count for the micro-VM, greater than 0. One of this field or the profile must supply it." |
      | "`memory`" | "ByteSize" | "from profile" | "RAM for the micro-VM, for example `512MiB`. One of this field or the profile must supply it." |
      | "`depends_on`" | "list<utf8>" | "none" | "Start order: VM or container names that must be ready before this container starts. Their provisioning is not waited for. `down` stops in reverse. No self-reference, no cycles." |
      | "`depends_on_provision`" | "list<utf8>" | "none" | "Provisioning order: VM or container names whose provision and playbook steps must all finish before this container's first step runs. Does not delay its start. No self-reference, no cycles." |
      | "`clock`" | "utf8" | "`host`" | "Guest clock policy, as on a VM. `host`: after an online snapshot restore the lab daemon sets the micro-VM's clock to the host's current time through the agent. `snapshot`: a restored container keeps the time it was captured at. An RFC 3339 date-time such as `2020-01-01T00:00:00Z`: the RTC starts at that instant on every boot and vmlab never sets it. Declared here only. See [vm and its children](ref_vm)." |
      | "`nic {}`" | "children" | "none" | "Network interfaces. None means air-gapped; exec and copy still work via the agent." |
      | "`env {}`" | "children" | "none" | "Environment variables passed to the container process." |
      | "`volume {}`" | "children" | "none" | "Host binds and named volumes mounted into the container." |
      | "`port {}`" | "children" | "none" | "Host-to-container port forwards; sugar for a segment `forward` to this container." |
      | "`healthcheck {}`" | "child" | "none" | "Health probe gating readiness. Without one the container is ready once its process starts." |
      | "`login {}`" | "children" | "none" | "Identities a person's commands run as on this container. Without one it falls to the user cinit resolves." |
      | "`provision {}`" | "children" | "none" | "wscript scripts run on `vmlab up` once this container is ready, interleaved with its playbooks in declaration order." |
      | "`playbook {}`" | "children" | "none" | "config-weave playbooks applied on `vmlab up`, interleaved with its provisions in declaration order." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    The image reference is parsed like a registry template reference: a
    first path segment with a dot, a colon or `localhost` is a registry host,
    and Docker Hub shorthand normalises to `registry-1.docker.io`. A digest
    must be `@sha256:` followed by 64 hex characters. The digest resolved at
    first pull is pinned in lab state and never re-pulled implicitly.
  DOC
  p "Validation enforces these rules:"
  list {
    li "The name is a DNS label and no VM or container in the lab has it."
    li "`image` is non-empty, has no whitespace, and any digest is well formed."
    li "`profile`, if set, names a known profile. `cpus` and `memory` both resolve through the container block, then the profile; a container that neither declares a size nor names a profile supplying one is an error rather than a guess."
    li "An `:idle` container cannot declare `entrypoint`, `command` or a `healthcheck`."
    li "A container with `port {}` blocks needs at least one NIC; forwards need a segment to reach it over."
    li "A container with `volume {}` blocks needs at least one NIC; volumes mount over the network from the segment gateway."
    li "Every name in `depends_on` and `depends_on_provision` exists and is not the container itself, and neither key forms a cycle across VMs and containers. A loop that crosses from one key to the other is allowed."
    li "`clock` is `host`, `snapshot`, or an RFC 3339 date-time such as `2020-01-01T00:00:00Z`."
    li "`login {}` blocks are judged against the Linux family regardless of the profile named: `elevated` is rejected and `password` is optional."
  }
  code wcl {
    filename    = "examples/mixed-lab/vmlab.wcl"
    source_file = "../../examples/mixed-lab/vmlab.wcl"
    anchor      = "container-web"
    dedent      = true
  }
  p "A dev container uses `:idle` mode, since it has no service to be, and a `login` for the account `vmlab shell` lands as."
  code wcl {
    filename    = "examples/dev-container/vmlab.wcl"
    source_file = "../../examples/dev-container/vmlab.wcl"
    anchor      = "dev01"
    dedent      = true
  }

  h2 "env {}"
  p "One environment variable passed to the container process."
  code wcl { source = <<'SRC'
env { name = "NGINX_PORT" value = "8080" }
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`name`" | "utf8" | "required" | "Variable name." |
      | "`value`" | "utf8" | "required" | "Variable value." |
  }
  p "A name that is empty or contains `=` is rejected."

  h2 "volume {}"
  p <<DOC
    A mount into the container: a host directory bound by path, or a named
    volume kept under the lab directory. Exactly one of `host` and `name` is
    set.
  DOC
  code wcl { source = <<'SRC'
volume { host = "./site" target = "/usr/share/nginx/html" read_only = true }
volume { name = "pgdata" target = "/var/lib/postgresql/data" }
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`host`" | "utf8" | "none" | "Host path to bind-mount, relative to the lab root. One of `host` or `name` is required." |
      | "`name`" | "utf8" | "none" | "Named volume kept under the lab dir, shared by name, retained until lab destroy. One of `host` or `name`." |
      | "`target`" | "utf8" | "required" | "Absolute mount path inside the container." |
      | "`read_only`" | "bool" | "`false`" | "Mount read-only." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Validation rejects a volume with both `host` and `name`, or neither. A
    `host` path must be a directory under the lab root. A `name` becomes a
    directory name, so it cannot be empty, `.`, `..`, or contain a slash.
    `target` must start with `/`. Named volumes are lab-scoped: they survive
    `down` and a per-container destroy, and only lab `destroy` removes them.
    Volume contents are outside snapshot scope.
  DOC

  h2 "port {}"
  p <<DOC
    A host-to-container port forward. It compiles into the same forward
    machinery as a segment `forward {}` and is installed against the
    container's lease when it becomes ready.
  DOC
  code wcl { source = <<'SRC'
port { host = 18081 container = 80 proto = "tcp" }
port { container = 443 }   # automatic host port
port { bind = "127.0.0.1" host = 18490 container = 80 }   # loopback only
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`host`" | "i64" | "automatic" | "Host port to listen on, 1 to 65535. Unique across the lab on the addresses it binds. Omitted: vmlab picks a free port when it installs the forward." |
      | "`bind`" | "utf8" | "`0.0.0.0`" | "Host IPv4 address to listen on, one the host owns. Omitted: every interface." |
      | "`container`" | "i64" | "required" | "Container port to forward to, 1 to 65535." |
      | "`proto`" | "utf8" | "`tcp`" | "Protocol: `tcp`, `udp` or `both`." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    A declared host port must be unused by every other `port {}` and every
    segment `forward {}` in the lab, and the container needs a NIC.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    An omitted `host` is automatic, exactly as an omitted
    `forward { host_port }` (see [Automatic host ports](ref_lab)): vmlab binds
    a free port when the container turns ready, [vmlab status](cmd_status)
    shows it marked `(auto)`, and a `vmlab container restart` keeps it while
    the lab daemon runs.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    `bind` works exactly as on a segment forward (see
    [Bind address](ref_lab)): an IPv4 literal the host owns, `127.0.0.1` to
    keep the port off the LAN, and `127.0.0.1` whatever it says under worktree
    isolation.
  DOC

  h2 "healthcheck {}"
  p <<DOC
    A probe run inside the container. Readiness is two-stage: the process
    starts, then the first probe passes. Only then does the container count as
    ready, and machines that name it in `depends_on` may start. Consecutive failures past `retries` fire
    `container.unhealthy`.
  DOC
  code wcl { source = <<'SRC'
healthcheck {
  command      = ["curl", "-fsS", "http://localhost/"]
  interval     = 10s
  timeout      = 5s
  retries      = 3
  start_period = 10s
}
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`command`" | "list<utf8>" | "required" | "Probe command run inside the container, in exec form. Exit 0 means healthy." |
      | "`interval`" | "Duration" | "`10s`" | "Time between probes." |
      | "`timeout`" | "Duration" | "`5s`" | "Per-probe timeout." |
      | "`retries`" | "i64" | "`3`" | "Consecutive failures before unhealthy." |
      | "`start_period`" | "Duration" | "`10s`" | "Grace period after start before failures count." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Validation requires a non-empty `command`, `interval` and `timeout`
    greater than zero, `retries` at least 1, and a non-negative
    `start_period`. An `:idle` container cannot declare one.
  DOC
}
